  flashcards: [
  {f:"Sanitation", b:"The lowest level of decontamination — removes visible dirt and debris and reduces pathogens to a safer level, but does not kill most of them."},
  {f:"Disinfection", b:"Kills most pathogens on hard, non-porous surfaces. Requires an EPA-registered product at the labelled dilution, kept wet for the full contact time."},
  {f:"Sterilization", b:"Destroys all microbial life including bacterial spores. In a salon this means an autoclave, and it applies only to metal implements."},
  {f:"Universal Precautions", b:"Treating all human blood and certain body fluids as if they were infected with bloodborne pathogens, regardless of what you know about the client."},
  {f:"Cuticle", b:"The outermost layer of the hair shaft — overlapping translucent scales that protect the cortex and must be lifted for permanent colour to work."},
  {f:"Cortex", b:"The middle and thickest layer of the hair shaft — holds melanin, elasticity, strength, and the disulfide bonds that chemical services target."},
  {f:"Anagen", b:"The active growth phase of the hair cycle, typically two to seven years. Catagen is the short transition and telogen is the resting phase."},
  {f:"Disulfide Bond", b:"A strong sulfur-to-sulfur cross-link in the cortex. Broken by a perm or relaxer's reducing agent and rebuilt by the neutraliser."},
  {f:"Level System", b:"A 1–10 scale describing the depth of hair colour — 1 is black, 10 is the lightest blonde. Level is depth; tone is the warmth or coolness at that depth."},
  {f:"Developer (Volume)", b:"Hydrogen peroxide in 10, 20, 30 or 40 volume. 20 volume gives one to two levels of lift; higher volumes lift more and damage more."},
  {f:"Sodium Hydroxide Relaxer", b:"The 'lye' relaxer — strongest and fastest. Applied to unshampooed hair on a based scalp. Never apply over previously permed or thio-relaxed hair."},
  {f:"Matrix", b:"The area of the nail unit that produces the nail plate. The lunula is the visible whitish portion of the matrix at the base of the nail."},
  {f:"Eponychium", b:"The living skin at the base of the nail plate. It may be gently pushed back but never cut — cutting living tissue is outside scope in every state."},
  {f:"Onychomycosis", b:"A fungal infection of the nail. A medical condition — decline the service and refer the client to a physician."},
  {f:"Patch (Predisposition) Test", b:"A small application of an aniline-derivative tint behind the ear or on the inner elbow 24–48 hours before service, to screen for allergic reaction."},
  {f:"Methyl Methacrylate (MMA)", b:"A liquid monomer the FDA considers unsafe for nail enhancements. Its use is prohibited or restricted in salons across the country."},
  {f:"Scope of Practice", b:"The set of services a licence legally authorises. A cosmetology licence covers hair, nails and basic skin care — never anything that breaks the skin."},
  {f:"Cosmetology Licensure Compact", b:"An interstate agreement giving licensees a multistate privilege to practise in other member states. Live in roughly ten states as of 2026, with more pending."},
  ],

